# The oracle references, pinned.
#
# One line per reference, tab-separated: <name> <image> <version> <what it
# answers>.
#
# **Every line names the same image**, which is not tidiness. These are not
# tools each answering about its own format; they are independent
# implementations asked the same question about the same containers, and the
# whole value is in where they disagree. In one image per reference, with one
# base state each, a disagreement would first have to be shown not to be the
# images' fault. One image makes "the reference set" one fact.
#
# The image was `...-tars` and its directory `containers/tars/` until zip
# arrived. Both are `refs` now: a name that says `tars` stops being true the
# moment a zip reference is installed beside them, and bsdtar is one reference
# answering about both formats rather than two references that happen to share a
# binary.
#
# The image is built here, so the tag is not a digest of the finished image: two
# builds of one Containerfile are not two copies of one image. What is pinned
# instead is everything the build reads - the base by digest, the packages by
# full apt version, resolved against a dated snapshot of the Debian archive so
# the pins do not expire - and the run-time version check is the real guarantee.
# oracle_env.check_pin() therefore runs in *both* modes; there is no stock image
# named here, so relaxing it in host mode would leave nothing checking anything.
#
# The version field is what the reference must say when asked. Three of them
# need a word about what the number is:
#
#   - For bsdtar it is the **libarchive** version as well as bsdtar's own,
#     because libarchive is what decides an answer and the two are not always
#     the same number.
#   - For python3 it is the interpreter version, because `tarfile` and `zipfile`
#     are stdlib modules with no version of their own and their behaviour has
#     changed between releases - 3.12 moved tarfile's default extraction filter
#     and 3.14 moves it again, and 3.14 teaches zipfile a method it can write -
#     so the interpreter's version is the module's. The two are separate lines
#     because they are separate references: a question about zip and a question
#     about tar that happen to be answered by one interpreter.
#   - **zip and unzip are two programs with two versions**, 3.0 and 6.00, from
#     two Debian packages, released eleven months apart in 2008 and 2009. They
#     are Info-ZIP's writer and Info-ZIP's reader and nothing makes them agree,
#     so a pin naming one of them would leave the other unpinned.
#
# Raising a pin is deliberate. A new reference version is new answers, and a new
# answer that disagrees is a finding to triage rather than a regression to fix.
#
# Building it:
#   make oracle-build

tar	localhost/ghoti-archive-oracle-refs:deb13	tar (GNU tar) 1.35	Writes all four tar formats; the corpus is generated with its --format flag
bsdtar	localhost/ghoti-archive-oracle-refs:deb13	libarchive 3.7.4	The second reader. A corpus written and read back by GNU tar alone measures GNU tar
pytarfile	localhost/ghoti-archive-oracle-refs:deb13	Python 3.13.5 tarfile	A third implementation, and the scriptable one: the member metadata every fixture is checked against comes from here
zip	localhost/ghoti-archive-oracle-refs:deb13	Zip 3.0	Info-ZIP's writer, which is what most zip files in the world were written by; its defaults are the extra fields a reader meets
unzip	localhost/ghoti-archive-oracle-refs:deb13	UnZip 6.00	Info-ZIP's reader, a separate program with a separate version; `unzip -t` is the accepted answer to whether a zip is valid
sevenzip	localhost/ghoti-archive-oracle-refs:deb13	7-Zip 25.01	A fourth implementation, and the writer for what the others will not write: WinZip AES, and a different version-made-by
pyzipfile	localhost/ghoti-archive-oracle-refs:deb13	Python 3.13.5 zipfile	The scriptable reading, as pytarfile is for tar - and the only writer here that will store an arbitrary member name

# Not here yet:
#   star      the fourth tar implementation. It reads formats the other three
#             do not agree about, and Debian 13 carries it; nothing has needed a
#             fourth opinion yet.
#   zipdetails Perl's zip dissector, which prints every field of every record by
#             name. It would be the reference for a *structural* disagreement
#             rather than a reading one - which record a value came from, not
#             what the value was - and nothing has needed that yet either.
